<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">French Congo</h1><p class="st-meta">1882 CE – 1910 CE (approx.) · capital Libreville</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">France's colony north of the Congo River, founded through Brazza's treaties and notorious for its concession-company abuses.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1880 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-savorgnan-de-brazza">Pierre Savorgnan de Brazza</a> signed a treaty with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tio">Makoko</a>, ruler of the Kingdom of Tio, placing land north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a> under French protection; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a> grew from his post. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a> of 1884-1885 recognised French claims, and the territories were united with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gabon-french-colony">Gabon</a> as the colony of Gabon-Congo, renamed French Congo in 1891.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-savorgnan-de-brazza">Brazza</a> was commissioner-general until 1897, ruling with few resources from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libreville">Libreville</a>. From 1899 the government handed most of the land to about forty concession companies, which forced Africans to collect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, leading to scandals and depopulation. A commission led by Brazza investigated the abuses in 1905.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1903 the colony was divided into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gabon-french-colony">Gabon</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/middle-congo">Middle Congo</a> and Ubangi-Shari-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, and in 1910 these were organised as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a> as capital. The concession system left a lasting legacy of depopulation and distrust.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-congo.jpg" alt="French Congo" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1882 CE – 1910 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Libreville</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pierre Savorgnan de Brazza, Émile Gentil</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Kikongo, Teke, Fang</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Republic of the Congo, Gabon, Central African Republic</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Congo francais</dd></dl></aside></div>
